-- allium: 3
-- liwp-ai-system-obligations.v2.allium
--
-- Lorem Ipsum Wealth Partners — AI Governance Policy, executable form.
--
-- Slice A of LIWP-POL-AI-001 (v2 amended). This file captures the
-- obligations the policy places on AI systems being deployed by the
-- firm: the entities a system must model, the controls it must
-- assert, the invariants its compliance posture must satisfy, and
-- the evidence (trace logs, model ledgers, material incidents) it
-- must produce. This is the file consumed by the firm's compliance
-- audit tooling when assessing a candidate AI system.
--
-- The companion file liwp-review-tool.v2.allium (Slice B) describes
-- the AI Governance Office's review process: submissions lifecycle,
-- reviewer surfaces, the deployment register, and the AI copilot
-- that assists second-line review. Slice B uses this file.
--
-- v2 AMENDMENT (December 2025): adopted in response to the FINRA
-- Regulatory Oversight Report tightening expectations on the
-- adequacy of stress testing for AI-assisted risk-management
-- systems. Five amendments are applied throughout this file, each
-- marked with the comment prefix
--   -- AMEND v2:
-- The amendments are:
--   B1. is_risk_advisory factor added to AISystem (§4.1).
--   B2. StandingScenarioSet entity added with annual review and
--       coverage obligations (§5.5, new).
--   B3. ad_hoc_scenario_review control added (§5.5) and required
--       for is_risk_advisory systems, with a per-scenario review
--       gate on the HITL arrangement (§8).
--   B4. ScenarioMethodologyLedger entity added to record what
--       selected each scenario, the data and calibration used,
--       and the model version that reasoned about it (§6.5).
--   B5. MaterialBreachIncident entity and reporting clock added
--       (§10.3).

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

value PolicyCitation {
    document_ref: String        -- e.g. "LIWP-POL-AI-001"
    section: String             -- e.g. "§4.1(b)"
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

enum RiskTier {
    low | medium | high
}

enum RegulatoryDomain {
    advice           -- §7.1: client recommendations
    | credit         -- §7.2: credit and lending decisions
    | privacy        -- §7.3: personal data processing
    | none
}

-- The vocabulary of controls the policy refers to. Names mirror §5.
enum ControlRequirement {
    -- Low and above (§5.1)
    documented_purpose
    | model_card
    | inclusion_in_deployment_register
    | basic_monitoring
    -- Medium and above (§5.2)
    | pre_launch_internal_review
    | defined_hitl
    | monitoring_plan_pre_launch
    | training_data_provenance
    | domain_obligation_mapping
    -- High only (§5.3)
    | independent_validation_by_mrm
    | pre_launch_red_team
    | incident_response_runbook
    | continuous_red_team_rotation
    | output_citations_traceability
    | governance_committee_approval
    -- Domain-specific (§7.1): Reg BI obligations for customer-facing
    -- advice systems. The Care Obligation (§240.15l-1(a)(2)(ii)) requires
    -- the firm to have a reasonable basis to believe a recommendation is
    -- in the retail customer's best interest. For AI-drafted client
    -- communications, the firm satisfies this through documented surfaces
    -- in the recommendation workflow.
    | reg_bi_fee_comparison_surface
    | reg_bi_account_type_comparison_surface
    | reg_bi_suitability_analysis_surface
    | reg_bi_nua_treatment_surface
    | reg_bi_conflict_disclosure_surface
    -- §7.2: Fair lending obligations for credit/lending systems.
    | fair_lending_disparate_impact_analysis
    -- §7.3: Privacy obligations for systems processing personal data.
    | privacy_lawful_basis_documented
    | privacy_data_retention_recorded
    -- AMEND v2 (B3): §5.5. When a risk-advisory system constructs
    -- or selects scenarios outside its standing set, each ad-hoc
    -- scenario must be reviewed by an independent qualified party
    -- before its results inform any consequential action.
    -- Independence excludes the analyst who initiated the session.
    -- A single end-of-session accept/reject does not satisfy this
    -- control.
    | ad_hoc_scenario_review
}

-- FINOS AIR Governance Framework risk identifiers, as adopted by the firm.
-- The hyphens require backtick-quoted literals.
enum FINOSRiskID {
    `AIR-OP-004`        -- Hallucination and Inaccurate Outputs
    | `AIR-OP-014`      -- Inadequate System Alignment
    | `AIR-OP-016`      -- Bias and Discrimination
    | `AIR-OP-017`      -- Lack of Explainability
    | `AIR-OP-018`      -- Model Overreach / Expanded Use
    | `AIR-RC-022`      -- Regulatory Compliance and Oversight
}

-- Finding categorisation vocabulary. ReviewerFindings carry one of
-- these categories. Slice A owns the vocabulary because the policy
-- defines what kinds of finding the review may raise; Slice B owns
-- the rules that actually raise them.
enum FindingCategory {
    tier_misclassification
    | missing_control
    | hitl_design
    | reg_bi_obligation
    | precedent
    -- AMEND v2 (B2/B3): stress-testing-design findings cover both
    -- missing standing-scenario-set obligations and passive
    -- ad-hoc-scenario review.
    | stress_testing_design
    -- AMEND v2 (B4): scenario run found without a methodology
    -- ledger entry.
    | scenario_methodology_gap
    -- AMEND v2 (B5): material breach not reported on the schedule.
    | material_breach_reporting
}

enum FindingSeverity {
    blocking | conditional | advisory
}

-- AMEND v2 (B5): breach classification, per §10.3. A material
-- breach is identified by what failed about the firm's risk
-- posture, not by what the AI system did.
enum BreachClass {
    exceedance              -- a documented risk-appetite threshold breached
    | tail_realisation      -- a tail-scenario outcome actually realised
    | correlation_breakdown -- a correlated hedge failed to hedge
}

-- AMEND v2 (B5): three-band materiality, retained from the v2
-- incident framework and reused for MaterialBreachIncident.
enum IncidentSeverity {
    minor | material | severe
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- Number of clients within a 12-month horizon at or above which a
    -- System counts as "broad-population" for tiering purposes.
    -- See Open Question OQ-Population-Scope.
    broad_population_threshold: Integer = 5000

    -- Expected reviewer time-per-output (seconds) below which an HITL
    -- arrangement is treated as non-substantive review.
    -- See Open Question OQ-Hitl-Minimum-Time.
    minimum_review_seconds: Integer = 15

    -- AMEND v2 (B5): §10.3 reporting clock to the AI Governance
    -- Office and Risk Committee, in seconds, measured from the
    -- breach being identified. Beyond this the firm is deemed to
    -- have missed the prompt-reporting obligation.
    office_report_clock_seconds: Integer = 86400

    -- AMEND v2 (B2): §5.5 standing scenario set review window.
    -- A standing set must be reviewed by an independent party at
    -- least this often (12 months).
    standing_set_review_window_seconds: Integer = 31536000
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The AI System being assessed. Holds the attributes that drive
-- classification under LIWP-POL-AI-001 §4.
entity AISystem {
    name: String
    business_sponsor: String         -- name/role of the sponsor
    technical_owner: String          -- name/role of the technical owner
    vendor_or_model: String

    -- §4.1 factors
    is_customer_facing: Boolean
    makes_consequential_recommendations: Boolean
    population_scope_twelve_months: Integer
    regulatory_domain: RegulatoryDomain
    finos_risks_engaged: Set<FINOSRiskID>

    -- AMEND v2 (B1): §4.1(g). The system is "risk-advisory" when
    -- its outputs inform the firm's risk-management decisions —
    -- running scenarios, computing exposures, proposing hedges,
    -- escalating breaches. Risk-advisory systems are subject to
    -- §5.5 stress-testing-adequacy obligations regardless of tier
    -- and regardless of customer-facing posture.
    is_risk_advisory: Boolean

    -- §4.2 default classification flag — true for back-office systems
    -- with no client outcome effect (presumed Low absent factors to
    -- the contrary).
    is_pure_back_office: Boolean

    -- AMEND v2 (B4): inverse relation to the scenario methodology
    -- ledger. Every scenario the system has run against is
    -- recorded here.
    scenario_methodology: ScenarioMethodologyLedgerEntry with system = this

    -- AMEND v2 (B5): inverse relation to material breach incidents
    -- recorded against this system.
    breaches: MaterialBreachIncident with system = this

    -- Derived
    is_broad_population: population_scope_twelve_months >= config.broad_population_threshold
    engages_material_finos_risks: finos_risks_engaged.count >= 2
}

-- The HITL arrangement described in a submission. The policy's §8
-- requires more than a passive-review claim.
entity HITLArrangement {
    submission: AIDeploymentSubmission

    -- §8: reviewer's role permits informed judgment about output substance.
    reviewer_qualified: Boolean

    -- §8: reviewer must take an explicit affirmative action to send or
    -- apply the AI output. The "for the avoidance of doubt" clause
    -- excludes arrangements where the AI output is the operational
    -- default and the human role is choosing not to override.
    requires_affirmative_action: Boolean

    -- §8: time available for review (seconds).
    expected_review_seconds: Integer

    -- §8: what the reviewer sees alongside the AI output.
    information_at_review_described: Boolean

    -- AMEND v2 (B3): for risk-advisory systems, the HITL
    -- arrangement must include a per-scenario review gate, not
    -- only a final-output gate. Without this, ad_hoc_scenario_review
    -- cannot be truthfully asserted.
    has_per_scenario_review: Boolean

    -- Derived: meets the policy's §8 standard.
    is_substantive:
        reviewer_qualified
        and requires_affirmative_action
        and expected_review_seconds >= config.minimum_review_seconds
        and information_at_review_described
}

-- The AI system's compliance posture: the tier the firm has assigned
-- it and the set of controls the system asserts it has in place.
-- Slice A models this as a static artefact; Slice B (the review
-- tool) layers submission lifecycle, status transitions and findings
-- on top.
entity AIDeploymentSubmission {
    system: AISystem
    submitted_by: String             -- name/role of the submitter
    submitted_at: Timestamp

    -- §4: a proposed tier from the submitter and the authoritative
    -- tier assigned by the AI Governance Office. The audit reads the
    -- assigned tier; if absent, no tier-keyed control invariants fire.
    proposed_tier: RiskTier?
    assigned_tier: RiskTier?

    -- The controls the submitter asserts are in place. The §5 and §7
    -- invariants below check this set against tier and domain.
    asserted_controls: Set<ControlRequirement>

    -- One-to-one (when present): the HITL arrangement claimed. The §8
    -- invariants below check substantiveness.
    hitl: HITLArrangement with submission = this

    -- Findings raised against this submission. The vocabulary
    -- (FindingCategory, FindingSeverity, ReviewerFinding) is defined
    -- in this file; the rules that *create* findings live in Slice B,
    -- as part of the review process.
    findings: ReviewerFinding with submission = this

    -- §6 lifecycle. The status field and its transition graph live
    -- here because they are structurally part of the submission, but
    -- the rules that flip status (Approve, Reject, etc.) live in
    -- Slice B — only the firm's review tooling drives those edges.
    status: pending_review | conditional_approval | approved | rejected | withdrawn

    -- §6: when the submitter justifies departing from precedent
    -- conditions because of materially differing circumstances, this
    -- flag captures that the justification has been documented.
    -- Slice B's PrecedentConditionsCarryForward invariant reads it.
    precedent_override_documented: Boolean

    transitions status {
        pending_review -> conditional_approval
        pending_review -> approved
        pending_review -> rejected
        pending_review -> withdrawn
        conditional_approval -> approved
        conditional_approval -> rejected
        terminal: approved, rejected, withdrawn
    }
}

-- A finding raised by the reviewer (or proposed by the AI copilot
-- for the reviewer to consider). The vocabulary lives here in Slice
-- A because the policy defines what categories and severities are
-- valid; the rules that actually raise findings live in Slice B.
entity ReviewerFinding {
    submission: AIDeploymentSubmission
    category: FindingCategory
    severity: FindingSeverity
    citation: PolicyCitation
    summary: String
    raised_at: Timestamp
}

-- AMEND v2 (B2): §5.5. Risk-advisory systems must operate against
-- a documented standing scenario set covering tail events,
-- correlation breakdown and liquidity stress for the desks they
-- serve. Ad-hoc scenarios are supplementary; the standing set is
-- the baseline. Annual review by an independent party is required.
entity StandingScenarioSet {
    system: AISystem
    last_reviewed_at: Timestamp
    reviewed_by: String
    covers_tail_events: Boolean
    covers_correlation_breakdown: Boolean
    covers_liquidity_stress: Boolean
    applicable_desks: Set<String>

    -- Derived: reviewed within the last twelve months.
    is_current: now - last_reviewed_at <= config.standing_set_review_window_seconds

    -- Derived: covers the three §5.5 dimensions.
    has_required_coverage:
        covers_tail_events
        and covers_correlation_breakdown
        and covers_liquidity_stress
}

-- AMEND v2 (B4): §6.5. Every scenario the system runs — whether
-- from the standing set or constructed ad-hoc — must be recorded
-- in a Scenario Methodology Ledger. The ledger captures what
-- selected the scenario (analyst or LLM), what data informed it,
-- what calibration was used, which model version reasoned about
-- it, and the independent reviewer who substantiated it (where
-- applicable). This closes the gap where scenario provenance was
-- previously carried only in session audit logs that did not
-- survive restart.
entity ScenarioMethodologyLedgerEntry {
    system: AISystem
    scenario_id: String
    selected_by: String              -- "analyst/<id>" or "llm/<model_version>"
    calibration_source: String       -- "historical", "implied_vol", etc
    model_version: String?           -- when LLM-selected
    reviewed_by: String?             -- independent reviewer, where applicable
    recorded_at: Timestamp
    justification: String            -- one-line rationale for selection
}

-- AMEND v2 (B5): §10.3 extended. A MaterialBreachIncident is the
-- artefact created when a stress scenario shows simulated exposure
-- breaching the firm's defined risk appetite — by P&L, VaR or any
-- other documented threshold. Classification drives the reporting
-- clock to the AI Governance Office and Risk Committee.
entity MaterialBreachIncident {
    system: AISystem
    scenario_id: String
    classification: BreachClass
    severity: IncidentSeverity
    occurred_at: Timestamp
    reported_to_office_at: Timestamp?
    reported_to_committee_at: Timestamp?
    summary: String
    citation: PolicyCitation

    -- Derived: reported to the Governance Office yet.
    reported_to_office: reported_to_office_at != null

    -- Derived: committee reporting is required when the system's
    -- assigned tier is Medium or High and the breach is at least
    -- material. The reporting clock for the committee runs until
    -- the next scheduled committee meeting (§10.3).
    requires_committee_report:
        severity in {material, severe}
        and DeploymentRegisterEntries.any(e =>
            e.submission.system = system
            and e.submission.assigned_tier in {medium, high}
        )
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- §5.1: every Low (or higher) tier submission must assert the Low controls.
-- Stated separately so the rules of §5 can be inspected one tier at a time.
invariant LowControlsRequired {
    for s in AIDeploymentSubmissions:
        s.assigned_tier != null implies (
            documented_purpose in s.asserted_controls
            and model_card in s.asserted_controls
            and inclusion_in_deployment_register in s.asserted_controls
            and basic_monitoring in s.asserted_controls
        )
}

-- §5.2: every Medium (or High) tier submission must assert the Medium
-- controls in addition. Domain mapping required only when a domain
-- applies (§7).
invariant MediumControlsRequired {
    for s in AIDeploymentSubmissions:
        s.assigned_tier in {medium, high} implies (
            pre_launch_internal_review in s.asserted_controls
            and defined_hitl in s.asserted_controls
            and monitoring_plan_pre_launch in s.asserted_controls
            and training_data_provenance in s.asserted_controls
            and (
                s.system.regulatory_domain = none
                or domain_obligation_mapping in s.asserted_controls
            )
        )
}

-- §5.3: High tier adds independent validation, red-team rotation,
-- incident runbook, citations on customer-facing outputs, and
-- AI Governance Committee approval.
invariant HighControlsRequired {
    for s in AIDeploymentSubmissions:
        s.assigned_tier = high implies (
            independent_validation_by_mrm in s.asserted_controls
            and pre_launch_red_team in s.asserted_controls
            and incident_response_runbook in s.asserted_controls
            and continuous_red_team_rotation in s.asserted_controls
            and governance_committee_approval in s.asserted_controls
            and (
                not s.system.is_customer_facing
                or output_citations_traceability in s.asserted_controls
            )
        )
}

-- §8: an HITL claim must come with a substantive arrangement.
invariant HITLClaimsMustBeSubstantive {
    for s in AIDeploymentSubmissions:
        defined_hitl in s.asserted_controls implies (
            exists s.hitl and s.hitl.is_substantive
        )
}

-- §7.1: customer-facing advice systems must carry the firm's Reg BI
-- surfaces in their outputs. The Care Obligation is satisfied by a
-- demonstrable workflow surface for each obligation; the spec uses
-- one ControlRequirement per surface.
invariant RegBIObligationsRequired {
    for s in AIDeploymentSubmissions:
        (
            s.system.regulatory_domain = advice
            and s.system.is_customer_facing
        ) implies (
            reg_bi_fee_comparison_surface in s.asserted_controls
            and reg_bi_account_type_comparison_surface in s.asserted_controls
            and reg_bi_suitability_analysis_surface in s.asserted_controls
            and reg_bi_nua_treatment_surface in s.asserted_controls
            and reg_bi_conflict_disclosure_surface in s.asserted_controls
        )
}

-- §7.2: credit and lending systems must carry the firm's fair-lending
-- analysis. Bias and discrimination is FINOS AIR-OP-016.
invariant FairLendingRequired {
    for s in AIDeploymentSubmissions:
        s.system.regulatory_domain = credit implies (
            fair_lending_disparate_impact_analysis in s.asserted_controls
        )
}

-- §7.3: privacy obligations for systems processing personal data.
invariant PrivacyObligationsRequired {
    for s in AIDeploymentSubmissions:
        s.system.regulatory_domain = privacy implies (
            privacy_lawful_basis_documented in s.asserted_controls
            and privacy_data_retention_recorded in s.asserted_controls
        )
}

-- AMEND v2 (B2): §5.5. Every risk-advisory system must operate
-- against a current standing scenario set covering tail events,
-- correlation breakdown and liquidity stress. "Current" means
-- reviewed within the last twelve months.
invariant RiskAdvisorySystemsHaveStandingScenarios {
    for s in AIDeploymentSubmissions:
        s.system.is_risk_advisory implies
            StandingScenarioSets.any(set =>
                set.system = s.system
                and set.is_current
                and set.has_required_coverage
            )
}

-- AMEND v2 (B3): §5.5. Risk-advisory systems must assert
-- ad_hoc_scenario_review and back it with a per-scenario review
-- gate on the HITL arrangement.
invariant RiskAdvisorySystemsRequireAdHocScenarioReview {
    for s in AIDeploymentSubmissions:
        s.system.is_risk_advisory implies (
            ad_hoc_scenario_review in s.asserted_controls
            and exists s.hitl
            and s.hitl.has_per_scenario_review
        )
}

-- AMEND v2 (B4): §6.5. Every scenario run by a risk-advisory
-- system must be recorded in the Scenario Methodology Ledger.
invariant ScenarioRunsHaveMethodologyEntries {
    for r in ScenarioTasks:
        r.system.is_risk_advisory implies
            ScenarioMethodologyLedgerEntries.any(e =>
                e.scenario_id = r.id
            )
}

-- AMEND v2 (B5): §10.3. A material or severe breach must be
-- reported to the Risk Committee within the reporting clock.
invariant MaterialBreachesReportedPromptly {
    for b in MaterialBreachIncidents:
        b.severity in {material, severe} implies (
            b.reported_to_committee_at != null
            and b.reported_to_committee_at - b.occurred_at
                <= config.office_report_clock_seconds
        )
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "OQ-Population-Scope: Does population scope alone (without customer-facing or consequential recommendation factors) escalate above Low? §4.1(d) lists it as a factor but the §4.2 defaults are silent on this case. v1 says yes via ClassifyMedium; needs confirmation from the AI Governance Office."

open question "OQ-Monitoring-Deferral: §10 says monitoring is 'a baseline requirement for production AI Systems' and one sentence later says monitoring 'may be deferred at the discretion of the AI Governance Office' for Low-tier systems with limited population scope. The spec requires basic_monitoring at Low tier without a deferral path. Which reading governs?"

open question "OQ-Equivalent-Assurance: §5.3 permits 'an alternative continuous evaluation arrangement that the AI Governance Office determines provides equivalent assurance' as a substitute for quarterly red-team rotation. v1 requires continuous_red_team_rotation outright. What evidence and decision process establishes 'equivalent assurance'?"

-- AMEND v2 (B4): OQ-Vendor-Change is partly closed. The scenario
-- methodology ledger records the model_version that selected each
-- scenario; a future revision should encode the threshold above
-- which a model substitution forces fresh review (FINRA 2025 calls
-- out vendor or foundation-model substitution explicitly).
open question "OQ-Vendor-Change-Threshold: ScenarioMethodologyLedger now records the model version per scenario, but the threshold at which a model or vendor substitution forces a fresh AIDeploymentSubmission review is not defined. Candidate: any substitution on a Medium or High tier risk-advisory system. Needs the AI Governance Office to set the rule."

open question "OQ-Hitl-Minimum-Time: config.minimum_review_seconds is a placeholder. §8 references 'time available for review' as a factor in HITL substantiveness but does not set a threshold. What time floor (if any) does the AI Governance Office set for advice-domain systems?"

open question "OQ-Default-Presumption-Override: §4.2 says the reviewer 'may depart from the presumption with documented rationale'. The classification rules in v1 do not model the override path. How is a documented rationale captured and what changes about the review?"

open question "OQ-Annex-A: §5 references 'Annex A — AI Control Standards' for the detailed description of each control. Annex A is reserved in the prose policy. The spec uses the bare control names from §5 as the ControlRequirement vocabulary; details should be added once Annex A exists."

open question "OQ-Domain-Mapping-Granularity: §5.2 requires 'domain-specific obligation mapping per §7 where applicable'. The spec satisfies this with the boolean control domain_obligation_mapping. A future revision should encode the mapping per domain (advice vs credit vs privacy) rather than a single tick-box."

open question "OQ-Exception-Path: §9 permits the AI Governance Committee to grant exceptions to §5 requirements on the basis of documented business case, mitigating controls and re-review date. The spec does not model the Exception entity, the carry-forward of mitigating controls or the re-review clock. Adding this would require an Exception entity scoped to a submission, with a status transition graph for re-review."

-- AMEND v2 (B5): OQ-Material-Incident-Reporting is closed by the
-- MaterialBreachIncident entity, the BreachClass / IncidentSeverity
-- enums, and the MaterialBreachesReportedPromptly invariant.

-- AMEND v2 (B3): a new open question raised by the stress-testing
-- amendments. §5.5 requires ad-hoc scenarios to be reviewed by an
-- independent qualified party, but does not yet specify what counts
-- as "qualified" for LLM-selected scenarios.
open question "OQ-Scenario-Selection-Independence: §5.5 requires ad-hoc scenarios to be reviewed by an independent qualified party before informing consequential action. The spec defines independence as 'excluding the analyst who initiated the session' but does not yet specify what counts as a qualified independent reviewer for LLM-selected scenarios. Candidate: a member of the desk's risk methodology function. Needs the AI Governance Office to set the rule."

